# Environment files — what each one is for.
#
# Copy the relevant block into the named file (all of them are gitignored).
# Every variable says WHY it exists, so a second machine can be set up without
# reading the source.
#
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# .env.development.local — read by `npm run dev` and `npm run tauri:dev`
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Points the app at the LOCAL Supabase stack (`npx supabase start`, needs
# Docker Desktop). Development never touches the real server or real people's
# data. These are the stock local values every Supabase install uses; the
# publishable key is printed by `npx supabase status`.
#
# VITE_SUPABASE_URL=http://127.0.0.1:54321
# VITE_SUPABASE_PUBLISHABLE_KEY=sb_publishable_...
#
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# .env.production.local — read by `npm run build` and `npm run tauri:build`
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Points release builds at the ONE hosted Supabase project that everyone
# (friends, family, both of your machines) shares. Without it, a release
# build shows "This build isn't connected to a server".
#
# The publishable key is designed to ship inside the app: row-level security
# on the server, not this key, is what keeps each person's data private.
#
# VITE_SUPABASE_URL=https://<project-ref>.supabase.co
# VITE_SUPABASE_PUBLISHABLE_KEY=sb_publishable_...
#
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# .env.admin.local — read only by `npm run invite`
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# The hosted project's SECRET key, used to manage the invite list. It bypasses
# row-level security, so it lives only on the machine you manage invites
# from. Never give it a VITE_ prefix: Vite inlines every VITE_ variable into
# the built JavaScript, where anyone can read it.
#
# SUPABASE_URL=https://<project-ref>.supabase.co
# SUPABASE_SECRET_KEY=sb_secret_...

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# .env — read only by the ui-ux-reviewer agent (.claude/agents/ui-ux-reviewer.md)
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# A LOCAL test account the reviewer signs in with. It exists only on your local
# Supabase stack (see supabase/README.md, "Local test accounts"); never put a
# real account or a hosted-project password here. No VITE_ prefix on purpose:
# Vite inlines every VITE_ variable into the built JavaScript.
#
# UI_REVIEW_EMAIL=ui-review@test.local
# UI_REVIEW_PASSWORD=<a throwaway password you choose>

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# GitHub Actions — repository secrets and variables (NOT a local file)
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Used by .github/workflows/release.yml and deploy-web.yml. Set them in the
# repository's Settings → Secrets and variables → Actions. RELEASING.md walks
# through them; both workflows fail with the missing name if one isn't set.
#
# Variables (Variables tab; not secret, they are visible in the shipped app):
#   VITE_SUPABASE_URL                 the HOSTED project's URL
#   VITE_SUPABASE_PUBLISHABLE_KEY     the hosted project's publishable key
#   WEB_URL                           optional: the live site's address, so a
#                                     deploy verifies it is serving the new build
#
# Secrets (Secrets tab):
#   TAURI_SIGNING_PRIVATE_KEY         contents of the update-signing key file
#                                     (`npx tauri signer generate -w <path>`).
#                                     Losing it strands every installed app.
#   TAURI_SIGNING_PRIVATE_KEY_PASSWORD  that key's password
#   VERCEL_TOKEN                      a Vercel access token
#   VERCEL_ORG_ID, VERCEL_PROJECT_ID  from `npx vercel link` (.vercel/project.json)
#
# Local release-style builds (`npm run tauri:build`) don't need any of the
# secrets: update files are only made in CI.
